            <h2>Common URL Slug Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
            <p>
              Avoid these frequent pitfalls when configuring web page addresses:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Changing Slugs Without 301 Redirects:</strong> Modifying a published slug without setting up a permanent 301 redirect immediately breaks every inbound backlink and bookmark, sending visitors to a 404 error page.</li>
              <li><strong>Over-Nesting Subdirectories:</strong> Avoid burying pages inside deep subfolder chains like <code>/blog/archive/categories/marketing/2026/04/slug</code>. A flatter structure such as <code>/blog/slug</code> is easier to crawl and remember.</li>
              <li><strong>Repeating the Category in the Slug:</strong> If your URL path is already <code>/tools/</code>, avoid naming the slug <code>tools-youtube-tag-generator</code>. Redundant words bloat the URL unnecessarily.</li>
              <li><strong>Relying on Numbers and Dates:</strong> Numbers in listicles change when you expand an article (e.g., turning "10 Tips" into "15 Tips"). Omit numbers from the slug to keep your URL accurate when content evolves.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>How to Update an Existing Slug Safely (Redirects and Canonicals)</h2>
            <p>
              Before updating an established URL slug, assess whether the change is truly necessary. If an existing URL already ranks well and receives backlinks, changing the slug introduces unnecessary risk for minor aesthetic gain.
            </p>
            <p>
              If you must update a published URL slug, follow these technical safeguards:
            </p>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Set Up a 301 Permanent Redirect:</strong> Configure a server-level 301 redirect mapping the old slug directly to the new slug. This instructs search engines and web browsers that the page has permanently moved, passing accumulated search equity.</li>
              <li><strong>Update Internal Links:</strong> Update navigation menus, header links, footer links, and related post links across your site to point directly to the new URL, preventing unnecessary redirect chains.</li>
              <li><strong>Update the Canonical Tag:</strong> Ensure the <code>&lt;link rel="canonical" href="..."&gt;</code> tag on the new page references the updated slug.</li>
              <li><strong>Refresh Your XML Sitemap:</strong> Submit an updated XML sitemap to Google Search Console to encourage prompt re-crawling of the new address.</li>
            </ol>

